    "clashSummary": "GRAMS vs MISSINK took place at a special **PenGame Rap Battle 2025** event, streamed live through **LiveJuice.tv**. The host frames the clash as a “fated attraction” battle, with the room expecting a mix of lyrical seduction, disrespect and personal tension.\n\nGRAMS goes first.\n\nRound one starts with GRAMS leaning heavily into attraction, lust and uncomfortable sexual humour. He says he and MISSINK could never be one, cannot “do up no fusion,” and then moves into family, drug-use, food, head, lust, bedroom and “Hadouken” imagery. His best material comes when he admits the whole thing is confusing because he can see a future when he looks at MISSINK. He gets a reload early and the crowd reacts to the awkward chemistry, but the round becomes loose and messy in places.\n\nMISSINK responds with a much cleaner first round. She immediately rejects the attraction angle, saying she would date a Black man, but not a Black man like GRAMS. She attacks his lust, lack of money, music, image, lack of street credibility, lack of demand from women, Asda job angle, domination/submission image and his mother struggling. Her round is direct, structured and lands more cleanly than GRAMS’ opener.\n\nRound two sees GRAMS continue with personal and sexual angles. He uses MISSINK’s real name, references her family, child, baby father, alleged drug-use around family, “Whitney” imagery, soul tattoo, Drizzy/Adonis angle, ancestors, witches and family racism. There are some direct angles, but the round still feels more scattered than controlled.\n\nMISSINK’s second is one of her clearest rounds. She attacks GRAMS’ body, height, sexual image, breath, image, nails, sexual performance, lack of bookings, dry phone, facial hair, being led by lust and needing alcohol to have sexual access. It is harsh, direct and easy for the room to catch. The “Peperami,” “Barney,” “origami,” “calendar for bookings,” and “virgin if alcohol didn’t exist” lines make this round feel fully controlled.\n\nRound three is where the battle becomes decisive. GRAMS begins by trying to drop the compliments and go darker, but the round collapses quickly. He stumbles, complains about the beat and mic level, and the host calls time. The room treats it as a choke or premature finish.\n\nMISSINK then closes with her strongest round. She attacks GRAMS through prison/spoon imagery, YouTube views, Instagram clout, being easily led, shaking hands with a man involved with his girl, two seasons with no motion, frozen flow, age/Instagram angle, following 19-year-olds, lack of substance, thirst, cats, food hygiene, fumbling chances and being “a light lunch.” The room reacts strongly, and the host calls it cooking.\n\nThere is no official judging section in the transcript, but the battle is clear from the footage and round structure.\n\nMISSINK wins by Archive Judgement.",
